Description: re-advertise the stateless HEVC V4L2 decoder on RPi builds
 RPi-Distro's new-in-151 rpi/v4l2-do-not-advertise-stateless-codecs.patch
 adds an IsStatelessDecoder() check inside GetSupportedV4L2DecoderConfigs()
 (media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_utils.cc): for every candidate device it issues
 VIDIOC_REQBUFS(count=0) on the OUTPUT_MPLANE queue and, if the queue reports
 V4L2_BUF_CAP_SUPPORTS_REQUESTS, `continue`s -- skipping the device so its
 codecs are never advertised.
 .
 On a Pi 5 the stateless HEVC decoder is /dev/video19, whose OUTPUT_MPLANE
 queue reports SUPPORTS_REQUESTS (REQBUFS capabilities = 0x1d). RPi added the
 skip because THEIR build only wires up the stateful decoder
 (V4L2StatefulVideoDecoder) and cannot drive the stateless path, so
 advertising HEVC there makes canPlayType() answer "probably" and then
 playback dies. OUR build DOES drive the stateless HEVC decoder (local
 patches 0001/0005 et al. wire up V4L2VideoDecoder + the H.265 slice-params
 delegate), so this suppression instead breaks working HEVC: canPlayType()
 returns "" and the demuxer rejects the stream with
 DEMUXER_ERROR_NO_SUPPORTED_STREAMS. This is the root cause of the 147->151
 HEVC regression (the patch did not exist in RPi's 147 packaging).
 .
 Gate both the IsStatelessDecoder() definition and its call site behind
 !BUILDFLAG(USE_V4L2_CODEC_RPI) so that on our RPi V4L2 builds the check
 compiles out entirely (no skip, no unused-function warning) and the HEVC
 device is advertised again exactly as in 147; non-RPi Linux builds keep
 RPi-Distro's original behavior. Owning this decision in our own local patch
 means any future RPi rebase that re-introduces or churns the suppression is
 either neutralized by this patch or fails loudly at patch-apply time rather
 than silently shipping broken HEVC. See
 docs/hevc-advertising-regression-151.md.
Author: chromium-rpi-hevc
Forwarded: not-needed

--- a/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_utils.cc
+++ b/media/gpu/v4l2/v4l2_utils.cc
